ZERO WEB DOM PROFILE 0.15 (EXPERIMENTAL)

Original implementation in Zero. This is a bounded tree builder used by the
experimental browser engine, not Chromium or a complete HTML5 parser.

Input and output

The caller provides the engine/declarations.zero.part ABI, a zeroed 4 MiB arena,
and a valid immutable disjoint input buffer of 0..65536 bytes. Literal NUL and
invalid UTF-8 are rejected before parsing. Pointer wrap is rejected; the worker
cannot establish that an arbitrary caller-supplied address is mapped memory.

At most 512 nodes are emitted in document order. Parents are earlier one-based
node numbers; zero means no parent. Nodes have a 15-byte lowercase ASCII tag,
63-byte id, 127-byte class and 255-byte inline-style field. The script type uses
the class field for script nodes. Ordinary text is split into at most 511-byte
UTF-8 chunks, never inside a scalar. Raw script/style spans refer to input bytes
and remain valid only while the caller retains that input.

Element and attribute handling

Names are ASCII-insensitive. Quoted and unquoted attribute values are accepted.
The first id/class/style/type occurrence wins, including a boolean occurrence
with an empty value. Supported attribute values decode the same entity subset
as text. Unknown attributes are ignored; href, events, async/defer and resource
loading attributes are not represented by this tree ABI. Every script src
attribute is explicitly rejected with error 65, including empty and boolean
forms, so fallback text is never executed as an inline script.

Comments and declarations are skipped. Void HTML elements do not become
parents. A slash on a nonvoid HTML tag does not close it. Closing tags pop
through the matching ancestor; unmatched closing tags are ignored. There is no
optional-end-tag insertion, adoption-agency repair, foster parenting or implied
html/head/body element creation. Such malformed or abbreviated HTML can produce
a tree different from a standards browser.

Raw script/style contents are scanned only for their own case-insensitive end
tag with a valid delimiter. Unterminated raw contents extend to EOF. Title and
textarea use RCDATA: their contents become entity-decoded child text, and tags
such as script remain literal text until the matching title/textarea close.

The contexts template, svg, math, iframe, xmp, noembed, noframes, plaintext and
noscript are rejected with error 65. Their inert, foreign or raw-text behavior
needs dedicated algorithms before scripting in those contexts is safe to claim.
This deliberately limited profile is not support for SVG, MathML or subframes.

Entities and errors

Text and supported attributes recognize lt, gt, amp, quot, apos and nbsp with
semicolons, plus decimal/hexadecimal numeric references. Zero and surrogate
references become U+FFFD. Unknown/invalid references remain literal and set
warning bit 1. There is no full HTML named-character-reference table or legacy
numeric control-code remapping. CRLF/CR normalize to LF outside raw bodies.

Error 65 means invalid encoding/syntax, an oversized supported field or a
rejected context. Error 28 means node capacity. On any parse error node count is
zero and partial nodes must not be published. A nonzero incoming error is left
unchanged and the worker does no work. Caller-owned buffers must not overlap
node storage, scratch, stacks or execution contexts.

Invocation and validation

The worker is created with fork. Switch to it once to prime its initial yield,
then once per document request. It uses no host services itself. Native test
wrappers allocate the arena with the original Zero host API.

dom-check.zero is composed from declarations.zero.part, dom-check-head.zero.part,
dom.zero.part and dom-check-tail.zero.part. Its 170 original Zero tests include
70 semantic/boundary cases and 100 deterministic malformed-input cases. Guards,
unchanged input checksums, node hierarchy and text bounds are checked each run.
The suite passed on Linux x64 and Windows x64 execution through Wine;
physical operating-system integration and full HTML5 conformance are not claimed.
